    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force keeps a list of the calls that were actually printed and, for every new call, rescans that list to find the newest print of the same message, which is O(n²) overall. The optimal version keeps a single hash map from message to the timestamp of its last print, so each call is resolved with one lookup and one update.",
      why_optimal: "Every call has to be inspected to produce its output entry, so O(n) time is a lower bound; one map lookup per call meets it. With at most n distinct messages the map holds O(n) entries, and the silent-rescan work of the brute force disappears.",
      pitfalls: "Writing `ts - prev > 10` instead of `>= 10` drops the call that arrives exactly 10 units after the previous print. Updating the stored timestamp on a dropped call makes later calls pass when they must fail. Forgetting that the first ever call for a message always prints, or indexing the output array by the loop counter while only appending on some branches, shifts every following entry.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rate_limiting",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_table",
    ],
