    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute-force approach compares every pair of strings by sorting their characters to check anagram-ness, yielding O(n^2 * k log k) time. The optimal approach uses a hash map keyed by the sorted character sequence of each string: all anagrams share the same key and land in the same bucket in a single O(n * k log k) pass.",
      why_optimal: "The hash map eliminates the all-pairs comparison, reducing time from O(n^2 * k log k) to O(n * k log k). On 22 test cases, PAF measured the brute-force at 89.4 ms and the optimal at 16.2 ms—a 5.5x speedup driven by eliminating the quadratic pairwise scan.",
      pitfalls: "Not sorting each group internally before returning produces non-canonical output that fails exact string comparison in the judge. Not sorting the list of groups by a canonical key causes order-dependent mismatches. In JavaScript, localeCompare must be replaced with a plain string comparison in Python to produce identical ordering.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anagram",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hash_table",
    ],
