    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force treats every clue as a question it answers by rescanning: for each filled cell it walks the other cells of its row, the other cells of its column, and the cells of the 3 x 3 box the grid lines cut around it, so a single clue costs O(max(R, C)) and the whole board costs O(R * C * max(R, C)) with R rows and C columns. It never stores anything, which is why its space is O(1). The optimal keeps one hash set per row, one per column and one per box, and asks each set a membership question before inserting: a clue that a set already holds makes the position illegal. On the classic 9 x 9 board that is 27 sets and 81 lookups for the whole scan.",
      why_optimal: "Every cell has to be read at least once, so O(R * C) is the floor and the single pass reaches it. Each set holds at most one entry per distinct clue per constraint, so 9 entries each, which is O(R * C) overall but only 81 labels on a 9 x 9 board. With R = C = 9 the win is not asymptotic noise but real work removed: the rescanning version performs 27 comparisons per clue, close to 2000 comparisons for a dense board, against 81 lookups.",
      pitfalls: "Forgetting that 0 is not a clue, so every empty cell collides with every other empty cell and legal boards are rejected. Computing the box index as (r / 3) * boxColumns + (c / 3) but with float division or with the wrong pairing, which silently merges boxes and hides real conflicts. Hard-coding boxColumns as 3, or dropping it entirely, which happens to be right on a 9 x 9 board and quietly merges or splits boxes on any other width. Walking a fixed 3 x 3 block of cells around the current one without clamping it to the grid, which reads a row that does not exist: a crash in Python and an undefined comparison in JavaScript. Reading board[r][c] once into a variable and then comparing the variable against itself, which misses duplicates. Mutating the board to mark seen digits, which corrupts the caller's input.",
    },
