    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force builds the answer by prepending each character to an accumulator. Strings are immutable in both languages, so every prepend copies the accumulator and the whole loop costs O(n²) character copies. The optimal copies the characters into a mutable buffer once, then holds two indices, one at the far left and one at the far right, swaps the pair they point at, and walks both indices one step toward the centre. Each swap finalises two characters, and the loop stops as soon as the indices meet or cross.",
      why_optimal: "The output depends on every character of the input and writes every character of the result, so O(n) time and O(n) space for the produced string are floor values. The two-index pass touches each character exactly once, which is the best possible. With n up to 200 the constant factor is invisible, but the index discipline is what transfers to longer inputs.",
      pitfalls: "An odd-length string has a centre character with no partner; the loop must use `i < j`, and it must not keep going after the indices cross. Writing the swap as two consecutive assignments without a temporary loses a character. In the brute force, prepending inside a loop is correct but quadratic, and reaching for it on a long string is the usual accidental slowdown; a single pass appending in reverse index order is the middle ground.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/In-place_algorithm",
      "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Reference/Global_Objects/Array/reverse",
    ],
