    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force enumerates all n(n + 1)/2 substrings (start index, end index) and tests each one with a two-pointer walk from both ends, so it costs O(n^3) time and O(1) extra space. The optimal solution flips the loop: instead of asking which substrings are palindromes, it picks the centre of a palindrome and grows outwards. A palindrome with an odd length is centred on a character, one with an even length is centred on the gap between two characters, so there are exactly 2n - 1 centres (n characters plus n - 1 gaps). While the two characters at distance k from the centre are equal, the slice between them is a new palindrome, so each matching expansion step adds one to the count and a mismatch ends that centre. Every palindromic substring has exactly one centre, so this counts each one exactly once in O(n^2) time.",
      why_optimal: "The answer itself can be quadratic (a string of 200 identical characters has 20100 palindromic substrings), and reporting it requires touching each one, so O(n^2) is the natural cost of any solution that counts palindromes by looking at them: with n <= 200 the 2n - 1 centres together expand about n^2 / 2 times, roughly 20,000 steps. Within that model the expansion is optimal, and it needs no table at all, unlike the O(n^2) time and O(n^2) space DP that stores a boolean for every substring. Manacher's algorithm does answer the same question in O(n) by inserting separators and tracking the rightmost palindrome's mirror, but that is a specialised refinement outside the scope of this bound.",
      pitfalls: "Handling only odd centres silently drops every even-length palindrome, which hides most of the answer on inputs such as \"aa\". Starting the odd and even loops from the same index without shifting the even centre to (c, c + 1) double counts single characters. Testing palindromes by building substring copies inside the expansion turns the work into O(n^3). The empty string must return 0 and the loops must not run at all for n = 0. Finally, expanding while s[lo] == s[hi] but checking bounds after the comparison indexes out of range, so the bound test must come first.",
    },
