    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force never builds a list of words. It first counts the words by re-scanning the whole string for every index it tries, then writes the answer by asking for word number W-1, W-2, ... , 0 from the left, and each of those requests walks the string from the beginning until it reaches that word. With W words each costing a full scan, the work is O(n · W), which is O(n²) on a string of a single long word repeated with spaces. The optimal pass walks the string once from the right with one pointer: skip spaces backwards, remember the index just past the last letter of the word, keep walking left to the first letter, and push that slice onto a list. Because the scan moves right to left, the list is already in reversed order, so joining it with a single space is the answer, in O(n) time.",
      why_optimal: "Every character must be looked at at least once to know which words exist, so Ω(n) is a lower bound for this problem and the single reverse pass meets it. With `s.length ≤ 200` the quadratic version is still fast, but it re-reads the same characters once per word, and that repeated reading is exactly the factor the one-pass scan removes.",
      pitfalls: "Splitting on a single space, which turns a run of three spaces into two empty words that end up in the output. Appending the separator after each word, which leaves a trailing space (prepend it, or join a list). Assuming the string is trimmed, so a leading or trailing space adds a phantom word. And returning \" \" instead of \"\" when the input is nothing but spaces, because the join of an empty list is the empty string but a hand-rolled loop can easily emit one space.",
    },
