    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force fixes a start index and walks to the right. While the walk has not met a zero the running product only grows, so once it reaches `k` no longer subarray from this start can qualify and the brute stops multiplying, but it keeps scanning because a later zero pulls the product back to `0`. The moment a zero appears, every subarray that reaches it has product `0`, so the whole remaining tail is valid in one addition of `n - j` and the start's walk ends. The optimal keeps a window `[left, right]` of positive values together with `lastZero`, the position of the most recent zero. Any subarray ending at `right` that starts at or before `lastZero` contains that zero, has product `0`, and is valid for every positive `k`: those are `lastZero + 1` subarrays, counted in a single addition. The starts strictly after `lastZero` see only positive values, so their product is monotone as the start moves right, and the usual shrinking window (multiply in `nums[right]`, then divide out `nums[left]` while the product is still `>= k`) returns the smallest start `left` whose product is below `k`, contributing `right - left + 1` more. A zero restarts the window at the index after itself, which is what keeps the running product from ever being divided by zero.",
      why_optimal: "Between two consecutive zeros each index enters the window and leaves it at most once, and a zero resets the window in constant time, so the scan is O(n) amortized: every element is read once and the two additions `lastZero + 1` and `right - left + 1` account for every subarray ending at `right` without enumerating any of them. Every element must be read at least once, so O(n) is the best any correct method can do, and the n <= 200 bound is what makes the quadratic enumeration legal to submit at all.",
      pitfalls: "Dividing by a zero: if the window is allowed to swallow a zero then `prod /= nums[left]` eventually divides `0` by `0`, which is `NaN` in JavaScript and a `ZeroDivisionError` in Python; the window must restart at `right + 1` when a zero arrives so the shrink loop only ever divides by a positive value. Treating the answer as `0` for every `k <= 1` is wrong as soon as zeros are allowed, because a subarray containing a zero has product `0`, which is below `k = 1`. The break in the brute force is only safe while no zero has been seen, since a later zero drags the product back to `0`; the brute must keep scanning for that zero rather than abandon the start. Count the zero tail as `n - j` subarrays, not `n - j + 1`. Reset `left` to `right + 1` on a zero, otherwise the starts at or before `lastZero` get counted twice, once through the zero term and once through the window term. In Python use `//=` rather than `/=` so the running product stays an integer.",
    },
