      pitfalls: "Two boundaries catch nearly everyone. First, leftovers: when the digits are non-decreasing the pops never happen, so the remaining deletions must still be taken from the back, as in `\"12345\"` with `k = 2` which gives `\"123\"`. Second, leading zeros: `\"10200\"` with `k = 1` is `\"200\"` and not `\"0200\"`, and an all-zero result such as `\"10\"` with `k = 1` must print `\"0\"` rather than an empty string. In the DP, `dp[0][0]` is the empty string and `dp[i][j]` must stay unset whenever `j > i`, or you will compare strings of different lengths and the minimum becomes meaningless. The leftover trimming has one more edge: it has to drop the whole stack when the number of remaining deletions is at least the number of kept digits, so that `k` bigger than `num.length` returns `\"0\"` instead of a slice counted from the wrong end.",
