    editorial: {
      approach: "Both versions first validate the space-stripped expression with one scan, tracking the previous character class and the bracket depth, and return -1 when the shape is illegal. The brute force then evaluates the way the grammar is written: it scans the expression, and on an opening parenthesis it rescans ahead to find the matching close, slices out the inner substring and recurses on it, adding the finished group value with the currently pending sign. Because a nested group is rescanned and re-sliced at every level, deep nesting costs O(n) work per level, so O(n^2) overall. The optimal drops the rescanning: inside each parenthesis the running total and the pending sign are pushed onto a stack, and the matching closing parenthesis multiplies the finished group value by the popped sign and adds the popped total.",
      why_optimal: "Each character is read a constant number of times and the only auxiliary storage is the stack, whose depth equals the nesting depth, so O(n) time and O(n) space are the floor for an expression that must be read end to end. The brute force pays the extra factor because it re-walks the interior of every group to find the matching bracket and to build the substring; the stack makes that pairing implicit, since the value between the brackets is already accumulated when the bracket is closed.",
      pitfalls: "Treating a minus as anything other than binary, which makes the sign bookkeeping ambiguous and diverges between the two strategies. Forgetting to flush the number currently being accumulated when a parenthesis closes, so the last operand of a group is lost. Applying the popped sign and the popped total in the wrong order: the group value must first be multiplied by the saved sign and only then added to the saved total. Validating the raw string rather than the space-stripped one, which wrongly rejects every expression containing a space. Letting a multi-digit literal be consumed one digit at a time instead of a run, and letting the running total grow without the modulus so the two languages round large values differently.",
    },
