    id: "bs-10",
    slug: "find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array",
    title: "First and Last Position of a Target",
    topic: "binary-search",
    difficulty: "Medium",
    pattern: "binary search for the lower bound and the upper bound",
    statement: "`nums` is an array of integers, handed over in any order. Sort its values in non-decreasing order and `target` then occupies a contiguous block of positions: those positions are the ranks of the target's occurrences. Return the smallest and the largest of them as the two-element list `[first, last]`, and return `[-1, -1]` when no value equals `target`.\n\nFor example `nums = [8, 5, 7, 8, 8, 10]` and `target = 8` gives `[2, 4]`, because the ordered values are `5, 7, 8, 8, 8, 10` and the three copies of `8` sit at positions 2, 3 and 4 once duplicates are counted from the left; the same array with `target = 6` gives `[-1, -1]`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200`, `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`, `-10000 <= target <= 10000`.\n",
    examples: [
      { input: "nums = [-10000], target = -10000", output: "[0, 0]", explanation: "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations." },
      { input: "nums = [4520, 8033, -6792], target = -8343", output: "[-1, -1]", explanation: "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations." },
    ],
    constraints: ["Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], target: number)`.", "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."],
    hints: [
      "The two numbers you need are the rank of the first copy of the target and the rank of the last one, both measured in the array's sorted order.",
      "The array arrives unordered, so create an ordered copy first; the rank of the first copy is the number of values below the target, and the rank of the last is one less than the number of values at or below it.",
      "In an ordered array each of those ranks can be found by halving: shrink towards the left on `value < target` for the first, and on `value <= target` for the second, starting with a high end of `n` so the position past the last element is representable.",
    ],
    solutions: [
      {
        title: "Brute Force",
        complexity: "Time: O(n^2) | Space: O(1)",
        language: "javascript",
        code: "function solve(nums, target) {\n  const n = nums.length;\n  let first = -1;\n  let last = -1;\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n    if (nums[i] !== target) continue;\n    let rank = 0;\n    for (let j = 0; j < n; j++) {\n      if (nums[j] < target) rank++;\n      else if (nums[j] === target && j < i) rank++;\n    }\n    if (first === -1 || rank < first) first = rank;\n    if (rank > last) last = rank;\n  }\n  return [first, last];\n}",
      },
      {
        title: "Optimal",
        complexity: "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
        language: "javascript",
        code: "function solve(nums, target) {\n  const ordered = nums.slice().sort((a, b) => a - b);\n  const n = ordered.length;\n  const lowerBound = (value) => {\n    let lo = 0;\n    let hi = n;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n      if (ordered[mid] < value) lo = mid + 1;\n      else hi = mid;\n    }\n    return lo;\n  };\n  const upperBound = (value) => {\n    let lo = 0;\n    let hi = n;\n    while (lo < hi) {\n      const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;\n      if (ordered[mid] <= value) lo = mid + 1;\n      else hi = mid;\n    }\n    return lo;\n  };\n  const first = lowerBound(target);\n  if (first === n || ordered[first] !== target) return [-1, -1];\n  return [first, upperBound(target) - 1];\n}",
      },
    ],
