    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force answers each query on its own by scanning the whole of `history` and keeping the write to the queried index with the largest snapshot id that is at or below the queried one; a later write carrying a smaller snapshot id does not override it, a write carrying exactly the queried snapshot id does, and equal snapshot ids are settled by position because the scan moves forward. That is O(n) work per query and O(n · q) overall. The optimal solution groups the writes by index once, sorts each group by snapshot id and then by position in `history`, and answers each query with an upper-bound binary search over that group: the last entry whose snapshot id is at most the target, which is the largest such id and, among equal ids, the one listed last. Seeding the search result at -1 is what makes an index with nothing written at or before the snapshot answer 0.",
      why_optimal: "Grouping and sorting the writes costs O(n log n) once, and each query then costs O(log k) for the k writes to its index instead of O(n), so the total is O(n log n + q log n) against O(n · q): a few thousand operations instead of forty thousand at the 200-by-200 maximum. The history has to be read once, so O(n) is unavoidable, and the sorting is what removes any dependence on the writes arriving in snapshot order.",
      pitfalls: "Assuming `history` is already ordered by snapshot id. The writes arrive in no particular order, so a per-index list is ordered only after it is sorted, and binary searching an unsorted list returns an arbitrary write instead of the latest one, which is exactly how the fast and slow versions stop agreeing. Taking the first qualifying write rather than the one with the largest snapshot id, and using a strict comparison on the snapshot id so the write made exactly at the queried snapshot is dropped. Forgetting the 'nothing written yet' branch, which must answer 0 rather than the earliest recorded value. Reading a history row as `[index, snapId, value]` when the first slot is the snapshot id, and a query row as `[index, snapId]`, since both rows are led by a snapshot id and the mistake is invisible until the answer is wrong.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Persistent_data_structure",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_search_algorithm",
    ],
