    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force compares squared distances and nothing else: copy the point list, sort it by the ranking comparator (squared distance, then lexical order, then length) and take the first `k` entries. That is O(n log n) time and a full copy of the input. The optimal solution never sorts the whole list. It keeps a max-heap of at most `k` points ranked by the same comparator, so the root is the worst of the current candidates. Each new point is pushed and sifted up, and once the heap exceeds `k` its root is dropped: the array's last element overwrites slot 0 and one sift-down restores the invariant. At the end the heap holds exactly the `k` smallest under the comparator, and sorting just those `k` entries produces the required order.",
      why_optimal: "Each point is touched once, and the only per-point cost that depends on more than the point itself is the O(log k) repair of a heap of size k, which gives O(n log k) time and O(k) extra space. At n = 200 and k near n the two approaches look alike, but the heap is strictly better whenever k is small, and it is the standard answer when the candidate set is a stream you cannot afford to sort. Linear-time selection by partitioning would shave the log k, but the constant factor of a hand-rolled quickselect on a comparator this fiddly is usually worse than the heap it replaces at these sizes.",
      pitfalls: "Comparing raw distances instead of squared ones is fine for ranking but invites an unnecessary `sqrt` and floating-point ties. Ties are the real hazard: equal squared distances must fall back to the coordinate-wise comparison, otherwise two correct implementations can return points in different orders and never agree. Off-by-one bites in the heap drop, where the last element must overwrite the root before the sift-down; assigning after popping the root's slot, or sifting before the overwrite, corrupts the heap. `k` equal to the number of points must return everything, `k` larger than that must still return everything, and an empty `points` list must return an empty list rather than throwing.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Euclidean_distance",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Priority_queue",
    ],
