    id: "ds-7",
    slug: "lfu-cache",
    title: "LFU Cache",
    topic: "heap",
    difficulty: "Hard",
    pattern: "hash map with a min-heap and lazy deletion",
    statement: "You are simulating the eviction policy of a Least Frequently Used (LFU) cache. The cache holds at most `capacity` distinct keys. A program issues a sequence of requests given as `accesses`; the request at index `i` (0-based) asks for the key `accesses[i]`, and its timestamp is `i`.\n\nFor every key the cache currently holds, keep a use count and the timestamp of its last request. A larger timestamp means a more recent request. Process the requests left to right:\n\n1. If the requested key is already in the cache, add 1 to its use count and set its last-request timestamp to the current index.\n2. Otherwise, if the cache holds fewer than `capacity` keys, insert the key with use count 1 and the current index as its timestamp.\n3. Otherwise the cache is full. Evict exactly one cached key, then insert the requested key with use count 1. The key to evict is the one whose use count is smallest; when several keys share the smallest use count, evict the one whose last-request timestamp is smallest, that is the least recently used among them. Append the evicted key to the result.\n\nA capacity of `0` means the cache can never hold a key at all: every request is a miss, nothing is ever inserted and therefore nothing is ever evicted.\n\nReturn the list of evicted keys in the order they were evicted. Return an empty list when nothing is ever evicted.\n\nBecause every request touches exactly one key, no two cached keys can share a timestamp, so the eviction choice is always unique.\n\nConstraints: `0 <= capacity <= 100` and `1 <= accesses.length <= 200` and `0 <= accesses[i] <= 100`.\n",
    examples: [
      { input: "capacity = 0, accesses = [0]", output: "[]", explanation: "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations." },
      { input: "capacity = 35, accesses = [97]", output: "[]", explanation: "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations." },
    ],
    constraints: ["Function signature: `function solve(capacity: number, accesses: number[])`.", "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."],
    hints: [
      "Each cached key needs two numbers, its use count and the timestamp of its last request. Trace a short sequence by hand and evict by hand before writing any code.",
      "The victim is the minimum under the ordering (use count, then timestamp). Rescanning the whole cache on every eviction costs O(accesses.length * capacity), which the bounds allow.",
      "For the faster version, push a fresh triple (use count, timestamp, key) into a binary min-heap whenever a key's numbers change, and discard any popped entry whose pair no longer matches the key's current pair.",
    ],
    solutions: [
      {
        title: "Brute Force",
        complexity: "Time: O(n * capacity) | Space: O(capacity)",
        language: "javascript",
        code: "function solve(capacity, accesses) {\n  if (capacity <= 0) return [];\n  const cache = [];\n  const evicted = [];\n  for (let t = 0; t < accesses.length; t++) {\n    const key = accesses[t];\n    let hit = null;\n    for (const entry of cache) {\n      if (entry.key === key) {\n        hit = entry;\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    if (hit !== null) {\n      hit.freq += 1;\n      hit.time = t;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (cache.length < capacity) {\n      cache.push({ key: key, freq: 1, time: t });\n      continue;\n    }\n    let victim = 0;\n    for (let i = 1; i < cache.length; i++) {\n      const a = cache[i];\n      const b = cache[victim];\n      if (a.freq < b.freq || (a.freq === b.freq && a.time < b.time)) {\n        victim = i;\n      }\n    }\n    evicted.push(cache[victim].key);\n    cache[victim] = { key: key, freq: 1, time: t };\n  }\n  return evicted;\n}",
      },
      {
        title: "Optimal",
        complexity: "Time: O(n log n) | Space: O(n)",
        language: "javascript",
        code: "function solve(capacity, accesses) {\n  if (capacity <= 0) return [];\n  const state = new Map();\n  const heap = [];\n  const evicted = [];\n  const less = (a, b) => a[0] < b[0] || (a[0] === b[0] && (a[1] < b[1] || (a[1] === b[1] && a[2] < b[2])));\n  const push = (item) => {\n    heap.push(item);\n    let i = heap.length - 1;\n    while (i > 0) {\n      const parent = (i - 1) >> 1;\n      if (!less(heap[i], heap[parent])) break;\n      const tmp = heap[parent];\n      heap[parent] = heap[i];\n      heap[i] = tmp;\n      i = parent;\n    }\n  };\n  const pop = () => {\n    const top = heap[0];\n    const last = heap.pop();\n    if (heap.length > 0) {\n      heap[0] = last;\n      let i = 0;\n      while (true) {\n        const left = 2 * i + 1;\n        const right = 2 * i + 2;\n        let small = i;\n        if (left < heap.length && less(heap[left], heap[small])) small = left;\n        if (right < heap.length && less(heap[right], heap[small])) small = right;\n        if (small === i) break;\n        const tmp = heap[small];\n        heap[small] = heap[i];\n        heap[i] = tmp;\n        i = small;\n      }\n    }\n    return top;\n  };\n  for (let t = 0; t < accesses.length; t++) {\n    const key = accesses[t];\n    const current = state.get(key);\n    if (current !== undefined) {\n      current[0] += 1;\n      current[1] = t;\n      push([current[0], current[1], key]);\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (state.size < capacity) {\n      state.set(key, [1, t]);\n      push([1, t, key]);\n      continue;\n    }\n    let victim = null;\n    while (heap.length > 0) {\n      const top = pop();\n      const known = state.get(top[2]);\n      if (known !== undefined && known[0] === top[0] && known[1] === top[1]) {\n        victim = top[2];\n        break;\n      }\n    }\n    state.delete(victim);\n    evicted.push(victim);\n    state.set(key, [1, t]);\n    push([1, t, key]);\n  }\n  return evicted;\n}",
      },
    ],
