    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force is a flood fill without a frontier. It marks `start` reachable and then rescans the entire adjacency list over and over, promoting any node that neighbours a reachable one, stopping when a full pass discovers nothing new. That is a fixpoint computation: one wave costs O(V + E) and a long path-shaped component needs O(V) waves, so O(V * E). The optimal version replaces the repeated rescan with an explicit frontier: a stack (DFS) or queue (BFS) seeded with `start`, into which each node is pushed at the moment it is first discovered and marked, so every neighbour list is read exactly once, O(V + E). Either way the last step turns the reachable set into the required edge list: walk only reachable nodes, keep a neighbour only when it is itself reachable and inside `0 .. adj.length - 1`, canonicalise the pair as (min, max), de-duplicate through a hash set keyed on the pair, then sort.",
      why_optimal: "Deciding whether a node belongs to the component requires looking at its adjacency list, so every node and every adjacency entry of the component must be touched at least once: O(V + E) is a lower bound and the frontier version attains it. The fixpoint version is a factor V slower because a node discovered late is only noticed when a later full pass sweeps past its predecessor. De-duplicating and sorting cost an extra O(E log E), which is dominated by the traversal.",
      pitfalls: "For the traversal, forgetting to mark `start` before the loop (so a self-returning edge adds the component twice) and pushing a neighbour without marking it at push time, which lets a triangle re-push the same node and can loop forever on a cycle. Reading `adj[start]` while assuming `start` is one of the nodes: when `start` lies outside `0 .. adj.length - 1` the row does not exist, so the first step of the traversal has nothing to iterate and the run dies instead of reporting an empty component. Emitting an edge from a reachable node to a neighbour that is not itself reachable: such an edge leaves the component and must be dropped, which is exactly what the symmetric input makes easy to forget. For the output, emitting an edge twice because the input lists it from both endpoints, or twice from the same endpoint when a duplicate neighbour is present, instead of de-duplicating through a set. Building the key as `u + ',' + v` without normalising the order, so `0,10` and `10,0` never collapse and a phantom edge `[10, 0]` appears. Sorting with the default string comparison, which puts `[10, 2]` before `[2, 3]`.",
    },
