    editorial: {
      approach: "Both versions start by labelling island A with a flood fill from the first land cell in row-major order, using an explicit stack over the four edge neighbours. The brute force then runs an independent BFS from each cell of A, stopping the moment it lands on land outside A, and takes the smallest (distance - 1) found. The optimal version seeds one queue with every cell of A at distance 0 and expands layer by layer through the grid; the first land cell outside A that leaves the queue gives distance d, and d - 1 is the number of water cells crossed, so it can return immediately.",
      why_optimal: "Multi-source BFS is BFS on the graph where all of A is contracted into a single super-source, so it visits each of the rows*cols cells once, giving O(rows*cols) total. The per-cell brute force repeats that entire traversal for every land cell of A, which is O(rows*cols) work per start in the worst case and O((rows*cols)^2) overall; on a 25x25 grid that is roughly 390k steps against a few hundred for the multi-source pass. Because every grid edge has unit cost, a BFS layer count is exactly the minimum number of cells to cross, so nothing cheaper than O(rows*cols) exists: the whole grid must be read.",
      pitfalls: "Off-by-one on the bridge length: the BFS distance to the landing land cell counts that cell, so the water crossed is distance - 1, and the adjacent-islands case (distance 1) must return 0. Starting the flood fill from an arbitrary land cell instead of the first one is fine only if the same island is chosen in both implementations; picking the topmost-then-leftmost land cell keeps the two consistent. Expanding into land cells is required (island A cells are already at distance 0, and reaching island B ends the search), so blocking land during expansion breaks the connectivity. A grid with no land at all must return -1 rather than crashing on the seed search.",
    },
