    editorial: {
      approach: "Give every cell a path length of 1 and sweep the whole grid repeatedly, extending a cell from any edge-neighbour whose value is strictly smaller: if that neighbour already supports a path of length L, the current cell can support L + 1. Repeat full sweeps until a sweep changes nothing, which is the Bellman-Ford relaxation idea applied to an acyclic graph, and costs O((m * n)^2) in the worst case. The memoized DFS removes the repetition: the answer for a cell depends only on its larger neighbours, so it never changes once computed. Cache it on first visit and each cell is expanded exactly once, giving O(m * n).",
      why_optimal: "Every cell must be inspected and compared against up to four neighbours, so O(m * n) time and O(m * n) space for the memo table is the floor; a cell that might extend a path cannot be skipped. The relaxation version needs no memo table but pays a multiplicative factor of (m * n), and the 30 by 30 bound keeps that small enough to run as a reference implementation.",
      pitfalls: "Using a non-strict comparison: a plateau of equal values must break the chain, and allowing equal neighbours turns a cycle into an infinite recursion instead of a finite graph. Reading the memo table as a boolean: a computed answer of 1 must still count as visited, so the sentinel has to be 0 and the check has to be a length comparison. In the relaxation version, stopping after one sweep under-reports staircases such as `[[3, 2, 1]]`, where the longest path runs against the scan order. Deep recursion can nest as deep as the number of cells, which a fully ordered 30 by 30 grid makes real.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Longest_path_problem",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memoization",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Directed_acyclic_graph",
    ],
