    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force builds the expanded graph whose nodes are triples (row, column, eliminations used) and finds shortest distances by repeated relaxation: the start state is set to 0, and the code sweeps over every state in raster order, relaxing all four outgoing edges in place, repeating whole sweeps until one sweep changes nothing. A move always costs exactly one, so distances grow by one per sweep and the process converges after at most m*n sweeps. The optimal version notices the uniform edge weight and drops the sweeps entirely: a FIFO queue pops states in non-decreasing distance, so the first time the goal cell is dequeued its recorded distance is final. That is BFS over the same state space, with a visited flag per (cell, budget) pair so a state is expanded once.",
      why_optimal: "There are m*n*(k+1) states and four edges out of each, so BFS spends O(m*n*k) time, which is linear in the amount of information the problem forces you to track. It cannot be asymptotically better: an answer may require exploring essentially every (cell, budget) pair, since reaching a cell with budget left over is a genuinely different situation from reaching it with none. A cheaper state (row, column) alone is not enough, and a Dijkstra-style priority queue buys nothing because every edge is already weight one. The m*n*k space bound is the price of not revisiting states. The budget itself needs no dimension beyond m*n: a shortest walk never revisits a cell (a repeated cell encloses a cycle whose removal shortens the walk and spends no more budget), so it never pays more than one unit per cell and any k above m*n behaves exactly like k = m*n.",
      pitfalls: "Three traps. First, the starting cell can itself be blocked: with grid[0][0] = 1 and k = 0 the answer is -1 even on a 1x1 grid, so the budget must be initialised from grid[0][0], not from 0. Second, budget is consumed per entry, so a walk that crosses the same blocked cell twice spends two units; solutions that track only the set of crossed cells disagree with this rule. Third, the budget dimension must be indexed for used values 0..k inclusive, and the visited array must be sized m*n*(k+1); sizing it m*n*k silently drops the all-free route and returns wrong distances. Fourth, the used count has to stay a non-negative integer: normalise every cell to a 0 or 1 entry cost and clamp the budget to m*n before sizing the visited table, since a shortest walk never revisits a cell and never spends more than one unit per cell. A cost or a used count that goes negative indexes outside the visited table, so those states are never marked as seen and the queue grows without bound until the process runs out of memory.",
    },
