    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force is a level-by-level BFS that carries the whole partial path inside the queue: it expands one full level, stops the moment any path has reached `endWord`, and therefore every path it returns has minimum length. The optimal version splits the two jobs. A first BFS from `beginWord` labels each reachable word with its distance from the start, and a DFS then follows only those edges that raise the distance by exactly one, rebuilding all shortest sequences from the small distance table.",
      why_optimal: "Storing the full path in the queue makes the brute force cost proportional to the number of partial paths it keeps alive, which can be exponential even when only a single shortest sequence exists, and it re-walks every stored word on each comparison. The distance map holds one integer per reachable word, so the BFS step costs O(n^2 * L) with n words of length L, and the DFS only visits words that can sit on a shortest sequence, which keeps the reconstruction proportional to the answer size T. Because no sequence may repeat a word, the level graph is acyclic, so the DFS terminates without any visited set.",
      pitfalls: "Forgetting that `endWord` must be present in `wordList`, and that `beginWord` is a legal starting point but is never used as an intermediate step. Continuing the BFS one level past the level where `endWord` appears, which lets longer sequences into the answer. Comparing words of different lengths and counting them as one letter apart, or counting the differing positions with a flag instead of a counter. Sorting with JavaScript's default Array.sort, which stringifies the arrays and compares their comma-joined text instead of the words themselves, and forgetting the `beginWord` equals `endWord` case where the answer is the single one-word sequence.",
    },
    readingLinks: [
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breadth-first_search",
      "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shortest_path_problem",
    ],
