    editorial: {
      approach: "The brute force stores the first operand as a little-endian array of bits and then processes the second operand one bit at a time: for every set bit of `b` at position j it performs a full carry-propagating increment starting at index j, walking right until the carry dies. One increment touches O(n) positions and there can be O(n) set bits, so a second operand like `\"111...1\"` costs O(n²). The optimal merges the whole thing into a single walk: two indices start at the last character of each string and move left together, adding the two current bits plus the carry, emitting one output bit per step and stopping only when both indices and the carry are exhausted. The bit array is then trimmed of leading zeros so the result is canonical whatever leading zeros the inputs carried.",
      why_optimal: "With n = max(a.length, b.length), the sum has at most n + 1 bits, so at least n input bits must be read and n output characters written; the single left-to-right merge does exactly that with O(1) work per column, giving O(n) time and O(n) space for the returned string. The brute force re-walks already-settled columns once per set bit, which is what pushes it to O(n²).",
      pitfalls: "Losing the final carry when both operands have the same length: `\"1\" + \"1\"` must be `\"10\"`, not `\"0\"`. Stopping the loop when the longer index runs out instead of when both indices and the carry are done. Forgetting that the operands may contain leading zeros, so the merge can emit a leading `0` that must be stripped, while `\"0\" + \"0\"` must still return the single character `\"0\"` and not an empty string.",
    },
