      why_optimal: "Both the brute force and the optimal version read the digits once each, and a number is fully determined by its d = O(log x) digits, so O(log x) time is the floor. The improvement is space: reversing only half the digits with integer arithmetic keeps the extra memory at O(1) and, in fixed width languages, avoids overflowing when the full reversal would not fit.",
      pitfalls: "Negative input must return false even though the digits themselves match. Numbers ending in 0 such as 10 are not palindromes, but 0 itself is, so that check needs the `x != 0` exception. With an odd digit count the loop stops with one middle digit left inside the unreversed half, so comparing `cur` with `rev // 10` handles it. Dividing a negative number with truncation instead of flooring, or comparing the whole reversal rather than the halves, are the other common slips.",
